>
  <article class="aip-article">

    <header class="aip-header">
      <a href="/american-idolatry" class="aip-back">← Back to American Idolatry</a>
      <p class="aip-kicker">★ AMERICAN IDOLATRY ★</p>
      <h1 class="aip-title">Don’t Tread on Me</h1>
      <p class="aip-subtitle">The Serpent America Hung on Its Porch</p>
      <div class="aip-rule"><span></span><span></span><span></span></div>
    </header>

    <div class="aip-body">
      <p>The yellow flag with the coiled rattlesnake hangs on porches, in garages, on pickup trucks, and in church fellowship halls across America. To most who fly it, it means freedom, courage, and a people who would not bow to a tyrant. Some have heard it praised from the pulpit as a godly heritage. Few have ever asked where it came from, who chose the snake, or what the flag actually says.</p>
      <h2 class="aip-h2">A Newspaper Joke</h2>
      <p>The story begins not in a church but in a print shop. In 1751 Benjamin Franklin, publisher of the <em>Pennsylvania Gazette</em>, ran a satire proposing that the colonies thank Britain for shipping its convicts to America by shipping rattlesnakes back in return. The snake was a joke. It would not stay one.</p>
      <h2 class="aip-h2">Join, or Die</h2>
      <p>On May 9, 1754, Franklin printed the first political cartoon in an American newspaper: a snake chopped into eight pieces, each labeled with a colony, New England at the head and South Carolina at the tail. Beneath it ran two words, <em>JOIN, or DIE.</em> The enemy then was France, at the opening of the French and Indian War, and Franklin was urging the colonies to unite.</p>
      <p>The cartoon leaned on a piece of colonial folklore: a snake cut in pieces would come back to life if the pieces were joined together again before nightfall. The severed colonies, rejoined, would rise as one living serpent. The image returned during the Stamp Act protests of 1765, and in 1774 it ran across the masthead of the Boston patriot paper, the <em>Massachusetts Spy</em>, now facing down a British dragon.</p>
      <p class="aip-verdict"><strong>The first cartoon of the American press was a serpent that rises from the dead.</strong></p>
      <h2 class="aip-h2">Franklin’s Emblem</h2>
      <p>In December 1775, writing as "An American Guesser" in the <em>Pennsylvania Journal</em>, Franklin made the case in earnest. The rattlesnake, he argued, was the fittest emblem of America. It has no eyelids, so it never sleeps on watch. It never begins an attack, but once engaged it never surrenders. Its rattles are separate yet joined so tightly they cannot be pulled apart.</p>
      <p>The man who chose the emblem is worth knowing. Franklin wrote in his own autobiography that as a young man he "soon became a thorough Deist" — one who believes in a distant Creator but not in the Scriptures as His revealed word. He was a Freemason, and Grand Master of the Masons of Pennsylvania in 1734. The emblem of the new nation was not chosen by a prophet or a pastor. It was chosen by a Deist and a Mason, and he chose a serpent.</p>
      <p class="aip-verdict"><strong>The virtues Franklin listed are the virtues of a watchman. He gave them to a snake.</strong></p>
      <h2 class="aip-h2">The Yellow Flag</h2>
      <p>That same winter the rattlesnake went to sea. The first Continental Marines carried yellow drums painted with a coiled rattlesnake of thirteen rattles and the words "Don’t Tread on Me." Christopher Gadsden of South Carolina, a delegate to the Continental Congress, gave Commodore Esek Hopkins a yellow flag bearing the same snake and motto, and it flew from the mainmast of the <em>Alfred</em>, the first flagship of the Continental Navy, in December 1775. On February 9, 1776, Gadsden presented the flag to South Carolina’s provincial congress, and it has carried his name ever since.</p>
      <p>A cousin of the flag, the First Navy Jack, stretches the same snake across thirteen red and white stripes. The Navy’s own historians say there is no firm evidence it ever flew in the Revolution; Hopkins’s only written description mentions a "strip’d jack" with no snake at all. Even so, the Navy flew it for the bicentennial in 1975 and 1976, ordered it flown again in 2002 for the war on terror, and kept it on the bows of the fleet until 2019.</p>
      <h2 class="aip-h2">Who Is Speaking</h2>
      <p>Look at the flag closely. It holds one figure and one sentence. The figure is a serpent, coiled and ready to strike. The sentence is "Don’t tread on me." The serpent is the one speaking.</p>
      <p>The flag’s spirit follows its speaker. Its snake never strikes first, but it never yields; its whole message is "touch me and I strike back." It is a creed of self-defense, pride, and retaliation — a nation pictured as a coiled serpent daring anyone to come near.</p>
      <p class="aip-verdict"><strong>Visitors see a flag of liberty. The flag itself shows a serpent issuing a warning.</strong></p>
      <h2 class="aip-h2">The Serpent Returns</h2>
      <p>After two centuries in the history books the flag came back to the street. Political libertarians took it up in the 1970s. The Tea Party movement made it its banner from 2009. It has flown at rallies of every kind since, it was carried at the Capitol on January 6, 2021, and it rides on specialty license plates and the jerseys of sports fans. Franklin’s cut serpent rides with it: "Join, or Die" is tattooed on the forearm of <a class="aip-inline" href="/american-idolatry/golden-age">the Secretary of War, beside his Crusader cross</a>.</p>
      <p>Many who hang the flag beside a cross never notice the contradiction. The meaning a people attach to a symbol does not change what the symbol is. Israel did not think it was worshipping a serpent either, until a king had to break one in pieces.</p>
      <a class="aip-card" href="/blog/serpent-underfoot"><span class="aip-card-head">What Does Scripture Say About the Serpent on the Flag?</span><span class="aip-card-line">Read the companion Bible study: The Serpent Underfoot</span></a>
      <p class="aip-closing"><em>"Come out of her, my people" (Revelation 18:4).</em></p>
      <h2 class="aip-h2">Record Consulted</h2>
      <p class="aip-record"><em>Pennsylvania Gazette</em>, 1751 and May 9, 1754. <em>Massachusetts Spy</em>, 1774. "An American Guesser," <em>Pennsylvania Journal</em>, December 1775. South Carolina provincial congress journals, February 9, 1776. Benjamin Franklin, <em>Autobiography</em>. Naval History and Heritage Command, "The U.S. Navy’s Jack." Historical summaries of the Gadsden flag and its modern use.</p>
